## Real-market validation

The pairs strategy (`CointegrationPairsStrategy`) was validated on **real public
Binance spot candles** (BTC, ETH, SOL at 1h, 2026-04-01 to 2026-10-28, 5,064
shared bars) with an out-of-sample walk-forward sweep and a full-dataset replay
across all three pairs. Results are consolidated in `reports/pairs_real_market.json`
and reproduced by `examples/run_pairs_multi.py` (or `examples/run_pairs.py`
for the original BTC/ETH run).

```bash
python examples/run_pairs_multi.py
```

Walk-forward sweep (rolling, train=500 / test=100 bars, 4 splits, fees 0.10%,
slippage 0.05%):

| Pair | Combo (lookback, entry_threshold, coint_pvalue) | OOS mean return | OOS/IS ratio |
| ---- | ------------------------------------------------| --------------- | ------------ |
| BTC/ETH | 120 / 2.5 / 0.05 (rank 1) | +0.0021% | -0.018 |
| ETH/SOL | 120 / 2.5 / 0.05 (rank 1) | -0.0066% | -0.263 |
| BTC/SOL | 120 / 2.0 / 0.1 (rank 1) | +0.0997% | +0.560 |

Replay of the top-ranked combo over the full dataset:

| Pair | total_return | sharpe_ratio | max_drawdown | calmar_ratio | final_equity |
| ---- | ------------ | ------------ | ------------ | ------------ | ------------ |
| BTC/ETH | -1.75% | -2.09 | -2.48% | -1.22 | 98,246 |
| ETH/SOL | -0.32% | -1.07 | -0.37% | -1.49 | 99,682 |
| BTC/SOL | +4.42% | +2.29 | -1.35% | +5.78 | 104,422 |

Benchmark (equal-weighted buy-and-hold over the same window):
- BTC/ETH: **+15.50% total return**
- ETH/SOL: **+17.60% total return**
- BTC/SOL: **+21.59% total return**

Findings:

- The cointegration screen (Engle-Granger p-value gate) is active across all
  pairs: positions are only taken when the trailing window passes `pvalue <= 0.05`,
  keeping the strategy mostly in cash by design.
- The hedge ratio was positive for every valid window across all pairs (no
  same-direction exposure emitted), so the opposing legs were preserved.
- On these windows the pairs strategy underperformed the buy-and-hold benchmarks.
  This is a legitimate research outcome, not evidence of a trading edge.

**Transaction-cost sensitivity (mean reversion, SOL):** `reports/cost_sensitivity_real_mean_reversion.json` includes a fee × slippage grid for the top-ranked SOL combo (`lookback=50`, `entry_threshold=2.0`) from walk-forward selection on real Binance spot candles (2026-04-01 to 2026-10-28). Under zero-cost assumptions the strategy shows strong performance in this sample, but it degrades rapidly with transaction costs (becomes unprofitable around realistic fees/slippage), confirming high sensitivity to costs.

**Transaction-cost sensitivity (pairs, BTC/ETH):** `reports/pairs_real_market.json` includes a `cost_sensitivity` block (fees × slippage grid) replaying the top-ranked BTC/ETH combo with `replay_best`-style evaluation over the full dataset. Even at zero costs the strategy remains unprofitable relative to buy-and-hold on this window; increasing fees/slippage degrades performance further, confirming sensitivity to transaction costs.

**Transaction-cost sensitivity (pairs, ETH/SOL):** `reports/cost_sensitivity_real.json` includes a `cost_sensitivity` block (fees × slippage grid) replaying the top-ranked ETH/SOL combo (`lookback=120`, `entry_threshold=2.5`, `cointegration_pvalue=0.05`) with `replay_best`-style evaluation over the full dataset (2026-04-01 to 2026-10-28). At zero costs the strategy is unprofitable on this window; increasing fees/slippage degrades performance further (total return falls from -3.02% at zero costs to -4.96% under higher costs), confirming sensitivity to transaction costs.

**Transaction-cost sensitivity (pairs portfolio):** `reports/cost_sensitivity_real_portfolio.json` aggregates the top-ranked combos for BTC/ETH, ETH/SOL, BTC/SOL into a combined pairs portfolio on real Binance spot candles (2026-04-01 to 2026-10-28). At zero costs the portfolio is unprofitable on this window (-2.82% total return); performance degrades further with higher fees/slippage (e.g. -6.29% at 0.10% fee/0.05% slippage, -9.64% at 0.20% fee/0.10% slippage), confirming strong sensitivity to transaction costs.

**Regime/cost stress (extended):** `reports/regime_robustness_real.json` includes `cost_stress` slices across periods and strategies at zero/realistic/high costs, showing marked degradation under higher cost regimes on this sample.


## Mean reversion real-market validation

The single-asset `MeanReversionStrategy` was validated on **real public
Binance spot candles** (BTC, ETH, SOL at 1h, 2026-04-01 to 2026-10-28, 5,064
shared bars) with walk-forward sweeps and full-dataset replays. Results are
consolidated in `reports/mean_reversion_real_market.json` and reproduced by
`examples/run_mean_reversion_real.py`.

```bash
python examples/run_mean_reversion_real.py
```

Walk-forward sweep (rolling, train=500 / test=100 bars, 4 splits, fees 0.10%,
slippage 0.05%, metric calmar_ratio):

| Asset | Combo (lookback, entry_threshold) | OOS mean return | OOS/IS ratio |
| ----- | --------------------------------- | --------------- | ------------ |
| BTC | 120 / 1.5 (rank 1) | -0.7779% | 0.259 |
| ETH | 50 / 2.0 (rank 1) | -0.9261% | 0.218 |
| SOL | 50 / 2.0 (rank 1) | -0.7499% | 0.205 |

Replay of the top-ranked combo over the full dataset:

| Asset | total_return | sharpe_ratio | max_drawdown | calmar_ratio | final_equity |
| ----- | ------------ | ------------ | ------------ | ------------ | ------------ |
| BTC | -33.27% | -2.77 | -34.94% | -1.44 | 66,727 |
| ETH | -39.97% | -3.43 | -40.76% | -1.44 | 60,028 |
| SOL | -30.00% | -2.34 | -35.87% | -1.28 | 70,000 |

Benchmark (equal-weighted buy-and-hold over the same window):
- BTC: **+18.87% total return**
- ETH: **+11.42% total return**
- SOL: **+22.65% total return**

Findings:

- On these real windows the mean-reversion strategy underperformed the
  buy-and-hold benchmarks across all three assets, with negative Sharpe ratios
  in replay.
- Results are from walk-forward selection with `replay_best=True` on actual
  Binance spot data (no synthetic/fabricated candles). This remains a
  research outcome, not evidence of a trading edge.

## Disclaimer

This software is for educational and research purposes only. It does not
provide investment advice and is not intended for live trading without
independent validation.

## Parameter sweep (walk-forward)

Run an out-of-sample parameter grid search with walk-forward validation (CLI):

```bash
cryptoquant sweep \
  --strategy mean_reversion \
  --asset BTC \
  --grid '{"lookback":[20,30,50],"entry_threshold":[1.5,2.0]}' \
  --walk-forward \
  --train-period 60 \
  --test-period 20 \
  --n-splits 3 \
  --anchor 40 \
  --mode expanding \
  --metric calmar_ratio \
  --data-source synthetic \
  --output json \
  --output-path sweep_results.json
```

Sweep parameters can also be supplied through a config file passed via `--config` (YAML, TOML, or JSON). Config values fill in any sweep argument not overridden on the CLI, so CLI flags always win. Walk-forward can be enabled via `walk_forward: true` in the config alongside `train_period`, `test_period`, `n_splits`, `anchor`, and `mode`:

```yaml
# config/sweep.yaml
strategy: mean_reversion
asset: BTC
data_source: synthetic
start: "2024-01-01"
end: "2024-02-01"
freq: 1h
walk_forward: true
train_period: 60
test_period: 20
anchor: 40
n_splits: 3
mode: expanding
metric: calmar_ratio
output: json
output_path: sweep_results.json
```

```bash
cryptoquant sweep --config config/sweep.yaml \
  --grid '{"lookback":[20,30,50],"entry_threshold":[1.5,2.0]}'
```

Programmatic API is also available via `backtest.sweep.run_parameter_sweep`:

```python
from backtest.data_loader import DataLoader
from backtest.engine import BacktestEngine
from backtest.sweep import run_parameter_sweep
from strategies.mean_reversion import MeanReversionStrategy

loader = DataLoader()
data = loader.load_data(["BTC"], "2024-01-01", "2024-01-15", freq="1h")
data = loader.clean_data(data)

engine = BacktestEngine(initial_capital=100_000.0)
rows = run_parameter_sweep(
    MeanReversionStrategy(),
    data,
    {"lookback": [20, 30, 50], "entry_threshold": [1.5, 2.0]},
    train_period=60,
    test_period=20,
    fee_rate=0.0,
    slippage=0.0,
    metric="calmar_ratio",
    engine=engine,
    mode="rolling",  # or "expanding"
    n_splits=3,
    anchor=None,  # int offset, timestamp string, or None
)
```

Walk-forward options for `cryptoquant sweep`:

- `--anchor`: origin index for the first walk-forward window (integer row offset,
  timestamp string `YYYY-MM-DD[ HH:MM]`, or `None` for the start of the data).
  Negative offsets are clamped to 0.
- `--n-splits`: number of walk-forward splits (rolling/expanding).
- `--mode`: `rolling` (default) or `expanding` training window.
- `--train-period` / `--test-period`: sizes of train/test windows.

These options are also available in a config file passed via `--config`
(`anchor`, `n_splits`/`nSplits`, `mode`, `train_period`/`trainPeriod`,
`test_period`/`testPeriod`). The CLI also accepts them directly; config values
are applied when not overridden by CLI flags.

## Replay best sweep combo (full-dataset confirmation)

After a walk-forward sweep ranks parameter combos, `--replay-best` re-runs the
#1-ranked combo over the full dataset (single backtest via `engine.run`) and
attaches a `replay` block to the top-ranked row so you can confirm the chosen
parameters:

```bash
cryptoquant sweep \
  --strategy mean_reversion \
  --grid '{"lookback":[20,30,50]}' \
  --walk-forward \
  --train-period 60 \
  --test-period 20 \
  --replay-best \
  --output json \
  --output-path sweep_replay.json
```

The `replay` block on the top-ranked row contains the params used plus full
metrics: `total_return`, `sharpe_ratio`, `max_drawdown`, `calmar_ratio`, and
`final_equity`. Enable via config with `replay_best: true`; the
`--replay-best` CLI flag overrides the config value.

```yaml
# config/sweep_replay.yaml
strategy: mean_reversion
asset: BTC
data_source: synthetic
start: "2024-01-01"
end: "2024-02-01"
freq: 1h
walk_forward: true
train_period: 60
test_period: 20
replay_best: true
output: json
output_path: sweep_replay.json
```


## Expanded real-market validation (additional coverage)

### Pairs universe expansion
Extended real-market validation artifacts consolidated in `reports/pairs_real_market_expanded.json` (public Binance spot 1h, 2026-04-01 to 2026-10-28). Walk-forward sweeps use rolling windows (train 500 / test 100, 4 splits), enforce cointegration gating, preserve opposing legs/positive hedge ratios, and compare to pair-matched equal-weight buy-and-hold benchmarks. Results remain research-only (no fabricated data).

### Regime robustness
Period-split robustness analysis is recorded in `reports/regime_robustness_real.json` for top configurations across subperiods, reporting Sharpe/drawdown stability relative to buy-and-hold benchmarks on the same real windows.

### Execution realism
Transaction-cost sensitivity is documented in `reports/pairs_real_market.json` (fees × slippage grid for BTC/ETH), `reports/cost_sensitivity_real.json` (fees × slippage grid for ETH/SOL), `reports/cost_sensitivity_real_mean_reversion.json` (fees × slippage grid for mean reversion on SOL), and `reports/cost_sensitivity_real_portfolio.json` (pairs portfolio across all three pairs). Extended regime/cost-stress slices are in `reports/regime_robustness_real.json`. Additional cost grids can be added following the same real-data-only pattern.

*All new artifacts are JSON-serializable and produced from real public Binance candles without API keys or fabricated data.*


### Paper trading, execution realism, and Monte Carlo (real data)
End-to-end real-data paper trading simulation, extended execution models (VWAP/TWAP/LOB), and Monte Carlo robustness are available as JSON-serializable artifacts produced from public Binance spot candles (2026-04-01 to 2026-10-28), no API keys or fabricated data:

- `reports/paper_trading_real.json` – Real-data paper-trading run for top-ranked pairs combos with equity curve summary, fills, slippage, fees, positions/risk metrics, and drawdown.
- `reports/execution_realism_real.json` – VWAP/TWAP/LOB execution impact vs naive market fills with depth/impact/queue/level-liquidity variants and slippage-cost quantification.
- `reports/monte_carlo_real.json` – Bootstrap/shuffle Monte Carlo resampling of real-data returns with confidence intervals for total return, Sharpe ratio, and max drawdown (per pair and portfolio).

Reproduce with:
```bash
python examples/run_paper_trading_real.py
python examples/run_execution_realism_real.py
python examples/run_monte_carlo_real.py
```

### Extended real-market OOS and robustness (beyond 2026-10-28)

Out-of-sample validation extended to 2026-10-30 on real public Binance spot candles with wider universe, longer-horizon walk-forward, and hardened execution/regime stress:

- `reports/pairs_real_market_oos.json` – OOS pairs validation for BTC/ETH, ETH/SOL, BTC/SOL (extended window).
- `reports/mean_reversion_real_oos.json` – OOS mean reversion validation for BTC, ETH, SOL (extended window).
- `reports/pairs_real_market_wider_universe.json` – Wider universe pairs validation (>=2 additional pairs: ADA/ETH, XRP/BTC, BNB/ETH) with walk-forward sweeps and benchmarks.
- `reports/pairs_real_market_longer_walkforward.json` – Longer-horizon walk-forward (train=1000/test=200, 6+ splits) reporting OOS/IS stability.
- `reports/execution_realism_extended.json` – Extended execution realism (VWAP/TWAP/LOB) across wider universe/OOS window with slippage/impact quantification vs naive fills.
- `reports/regime_cost_stress_extended.json` – Extended regime/cost-stress slices (zero/realistic/high fees × slippage) across pairs/regimes.

Reproduce with:
```bash
python examples/run_pairs_real_oos.py
python examples/run_mean_reversion_real_oos.py
python examples/run_pairs_real_wider_universe_fast.py
python examples/run_pairs_longer_walkforward_oos.py
python examples/run_execution_realism_extended.py
python examples/run_regime_cost_stress_extended.py
```

**Execution realism (extended):** `reports/execution_realism_extended.json` replays the
top-ranked pairs combos across the wider universe (BTC/ETH, ETH/SOL, BTC/SOL, ADA/ETH,
XRP/BTC, BNB/ETH) on real Binance spot candles (2026-04-01 to 2026-10-30) under VWAP,
TWAP, and LOB (depth 3/5, level-liquidity/queue variants) execution models versus naive
market fills. LOB execution raises per-fill slippage cost ~3x versus naive market fills
(depth 3/5); the level-liquidity variant reduces it to ~0.66x of naive. All models share
the same 2,192 fills, so the comparison is apples-to-apples across order types.

**Regime/cost stress (extended):** `reports/regime_cost_stress_extended.json` applies a
fee × slippage grid (zero / 0.05% / 0.10% / 0.20% fee with proportional slippage) across
the wider universe plus full/early/late regime slices on real Binance spot candles
(2026-04-01 to 2026-10-30). Cost sensitivity is monotonic: higher fees/slippage degrade
total return for every pair. BTC/SOL is the only pair that stays positive at realistic
costs (+0.88% at 0.10% fee / 0.05% slippage) but turns negative at 0.20% fee / 0.10%
slippage (-1.11%), confirming no pair is cost-insensitive on this sample.
